{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הרעתה", :translit "hara'otah", :gloss "you have done evil"} {:he "למה הרעת לעבדך", :translit "lamah hara'ota la'avdekha", :gloss "why have you done evil to your servant"} {:he "עתה תראה", :translit "atah tir'eh", :gloss "now you shall see"} {:he "עתה תראה היקרך דברי", :translit "atah tir'eh hiyakarkha devarai", :gloss "now you shall see the preciousness of my words"} {:he "הרעת להמית את בנה", :translit "hara'ot lehamit et benah", :gloss "you have done evil to kill her son"} {:he "על ת למרום שבית שבי", :translit "al tet la'marom shevit shevi", :gloss "do not set on high the captivity of your donkey"} {:he "ויעל אליהו בסערה השמימה", :translit "va'ya'al eliyah bi'se'arah ha'shamaymah", :gloss "and Elijah went up in a storm to heaven"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הרעתה" "למה הרעת לעבדך" "הרעת להמית את בנה"]} {:kind :other, :note "Verbal link עתה תראה — Exodus 5:23 and Numbers 20:24 (or Deut 3:25-26) — both mark Moses denied entry to the Land."} {:kind :other, :note "Parallel between Moses (Exodus 4:24) and Elijah (1 Kings 17): in both a son nearly dies and is saved by prayer."} {:kind :other, :note "Parallel ascent motif: Moses: על ת למרום שבית שבי (Exodus 23:12, via midrashic reading על-ת / שבית-שבי); Elijah: ויעל אליהו בסערה השמימה (2 Kings 2:11)."}], :frame {:text "At a lodging place on the road, the LORD encountered Moses (or his son) and sought to kill him; the reading involves Exodus 4:24. The midrash identifies the threatened party as Moses's son, saved by Moses's prayer (Tanchuma Yitro 21; Rashi on Exodus 4:24 citing Shemot Rabbah). The prohibition 'do not set on high the captivity of your donkey' (Exodus 23:12) is read midrashically as forbidding ascent while a fellow is in captivity (Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael, Mishpatim, on Exodus 23:12), connecting to Moses's own not ascending into the Land. Elijah revived the widow's son through prayer (1 Kings 17:21-22) and ascended in a storm to heaven (2 Kings 2:11).", :sources ["Exodus 4:24" "Exodus 23:12" "1 Kings 17:21-22" "2 Kings 2:11" "Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael Mishpatim on Exodus 23:12" "Tanchuma Yitro 21" "Rashi on Exodus 4:24"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הרעתה" "למה הרעת לעבדך" "הרעת להמית את בנה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "הרעתה", :refs ["Exodus 5:22"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "למה הרעת לעבדך", :refs ["Numbers 11:11"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "הרעת להמית את בנה", :refs ["1 Kings 17:20"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Verbal link עתה תראה — Exodus 5:23 and Numbers 20:24 (or Deut 3:25-26) — both mark Moses denied entry to the Land.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Parallel between Moses (Exodus 4:24) and Elijah (1 Kings 17): in both a son nearly dies and is saved by prayer.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Parallel ascent motif: Moses: על ת למרום שבית שבי (Exodus 23:12, via midrashic reading על-ת / שבית-שבי); Elijah: ויעל אליהו בסערה השמימה (2 Kings 2:11).", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:14:04.656803514Z", :model "glm-5.2"}